{"uuid":"a8448367-8aaa-4485-8f58-0f8e0ad840db","version":1,"name":"Does LLM-generated code smell?","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly being used to generate program code. Much research has been reported on the functional correctness of generated code, but there is far less on code quality. In this study, we evaluate the code smells, an important indicator of code quality, of the Java programs generated in response to prompts given to four state-of-the-art LLMs: Gemini Pro, ChatGPT, Codex, and Falcon. We find that LLM-generated code has a higher incidence of code smells compared to reference solutions and this number increases with coding task complexity. We also find that the types of code smells vary with both programming topic and the choice of model. Falcon performed best, followed by Gemini Pro and ChatGPT. Codex performed worst, increasing by 109% the average number of violations of coding rules. The quality of LLM-generated code tends to be worse in advanced topics such as encapsulation, abstract class and generics, polymorphism and inheritance, etc. The worst types of code smells in the baseline were also the worst in the LLM-generated code. On average across all LLMs, the implementation smells increase by 81 % and design smells by 64 %.","metadata":"<xml><item><datacite><dates><obu_embargoEnd>2026-10-17</obu_embargoEnd><obu_otherAction>none</obu_otherAction></dates><titles><title titleType=\"MainTitle\">Does LLM-generated code smell?</title></titles><descriptions><description descriptionType=\"Abstract\">Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly being used to generate program code. Much research has been reported on the functional correctness of generated code, but there is far less on code quality. In this study, we evaluate the code smells, an important indicator of code quality, of the Java programs generated in response to prompts given to four state-of-the-art LLMs: Gemini Pro, ChatGPT, Codex, and Falcon. We find that LLM-generated code has a higher incidence of code smells compared to reference solutions and this number increases with coding task complexity. We also find that the types of code smells vary with both programming topic and the choice of model. Falcon performed best, followed by Gemini Pro and ChatGPT. Codex performed worst, increasing by 109% the average number of violations of coding rules. The quality of LLM-generated code tends to be worse in advanced topics such as encapsulation, abstract class and generics, polymorphism and inheritance, etc. The worst types of code smells in the baseline were also the worst in the LLM-generated code. On average across all LLMs, the implementation smells increase by 81 % and design smells by 64 %.</description></descriptions><creators><obu_creatorList>Paul, Debalina Ghosh; Zhu, Hong; Bayley, Ian; </obu_creatorList><creator><creatorName nameType=\"Personal\">Paul, Debalina Ghosh</creatorName></creator><creator><creatorName nameType=\"Personal\">Zhu, Hong</creatorName><nameIdentifier>0000-0001-9393-5005</nameIdentifier></creator><creator><creatorName nameType=\"Personal\">Bayley, Ian</creatorName></creator></creators><rightsList><obu_generalRights><generalRights>postEmbargoOpenAccess</generalRights></obu_generalRights><rights><obu_rightsName>CustomStatement</obu_rightsName><obu_rightsText>&quot;&#x00a9; 2026 IEEE. Personal use of this material is permitted.  Permission from IEEE must be obtained for all other uses, in any current or future media, including reprinting/republishing this material for advertising or promotional purposes, creating new collective works, for resale or redistribution to servers or lists, or reuse of any copyrighted component of this work in other works.&#x201d;</obu_rightsText></rights></rightsList><relatedIdentifiers><relatedIdentifier><obu_id relatedIdentifierType=\"DOI\" relationType=\"IsVersionOf\">10.1109/ICCBDC67784.2025.00017</obu_id><obu_citation><citationTitle>Does LLM Generated Code Smell?</citationTitle></obu_citation></relatedIdentifier></relatedIdentifiers><resourceType>conference paper</resourceType><publicationYear>2026</publicationYear><formats><format>application/pdf</format></formats><version>AM</version><identifier/></datacite><obu><attachmentsUUIDs><attachmentUUID>4b820017-ad36-4206-b3bc-7613e1675af1</attachmentUUID></attachmentsUUIDs><depositorAgreement>depositedByAdmin</depositorAgreement><notes>Conference dates: 2025.08.14-16\nCheck: https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/Xplore/home.jsp\nThis might be Bayley: check IEEE for confimration: https://orcid.org/0009-0001-8934-5376\n2025.09.03: NYP\n2025.10.08: NYP\n2025.11.06: NYP\n2025.12.05: NYP\n2026.01.05: NYP\n2026.02.05: NYP\n2026.03.05: NYP\n2026.04.02: NYP\n2026.05.05: published.</notes></obu><otherSchema><uketdterms_departments><uketdterms_department>School of Engineering, Computing and Mathematics</uketdterms_department></uketdterms_departments></otherSchema></item></xml>","status":"live","createdDate":"2025-07-17T10:28:04.166Z","modifiedDate":"2026-10-19T08:23:43.859Z","owner":{"id":"p0084952"},"collaborators":[],"collection":{"uuid":"afee126f-04b2-41a9-a6dd-b29b7c6c20ab"},"rating":-1.0,"attachments":[{"type":"file","uuid":"4b820017-ad36-4206-b3bc-7613e1675af1","description":"Does LLM generated code smell - 2025 - Paul Zhu Bayley.pdf","preview":false,"erroredIndexing":false,"restricted":false,"thumbnail":"_THUMBS/Does LLM generated code smell - 2025 - Paul Zhu Bayley.pdf.jpeg","filename":"Does LLM generated code smell - 2025 - Paul Zhu Bayley.pdf","size":284317,"md5":"074d190833c6cfd37bcebb7a5e25af70","conversion":false,"thumbFilename":"_THUMBS/Does LLM generated code smell - 2025 - Paul Zhu Bayley.pdf.jpeg","links":{"view":"https://radar.brookes.ac.uk/radar/items/a8448367-8aaa-4485-8f58-0f8e0ad840db/1/?attachment.uuid=4b820017-ad36-4206-b3bc-7613e1675af1","thumbnail":"https://radar.brookes.ac.uk/radar/thumbs/a8448367-8aaa-4485-8f58-0f8e0ad840db/1/4b820017-ad36-4206-b3bc-7613e1675af1"}}],"navigation":{